c3e246a6259dc6c24b0acefa","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0004","evidenceText":"By using the same HEC-RAS derived discharges and FIM extents for creating maps with OWP FIM, we are able to separate out errors introduced by NWM inputs and processes including land surface interactions, groundwater fluxes, atmospheric forcings, hydraulic routing, and others that would have potentially affected our conclusions if we had used NWM forecasted discharges.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":78501,"startOffsetInUnit":3249},{"endOffsetInDocument":79171,"endOffsetInUnit":3919,"evidenceHash":"0295898cab5a07c18d032b5b2b887a02fca54d77a2f9b69dd372fa4a76a5ce5a","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0005","evidenceText":"We elected to spatially intersect the HEC-RAS cross sections with the NWM stream network assigning the 1% and 0.2% flow rates to each NWM reach.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":79027,"startOffsetInUnit":3775},{"endOffsetInDocument":76967,"endOffsetInUnit":1715,"evidenceHash":"5fef205297552581f5c1ff4e4ceb5831d4c0174431fabf8ad11efc2090b516fc","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0006","evidenceText":"Due to this fact, evaluation of our relative elevation CFIM method was conducted by comparison to the HEC-RAS 1D models produced within FEMA region 6","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":76818,"startOffsetInUnit":1566},{"endOffsetInDocument":76817,"endOffsetInUnit":1565,"evidenceHash":"5942761bbe26d2bb98ec8706b39f05cb6a1ef181e2e1e733a05ef39dc1343982","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0007","evidenceText":"Our HAND based approach coupled with SRCs requires streamflow as input and is agnostic as to the source of that streamflow whether forecasted, observed, or probablistic.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":76648,"startOffsetInUnit":1396},{"endOffsetInDocument":76468,"endOffsetInUnit":1216,"evidenceHash":"bd89b3134de6977e2a5defe5c5a06c672e7839a1ea7bbd1f93c2768b24201fa0","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0008","evidenceText":"On the other hand, relying on point sources of streamflow or stage, such as gages, would eliminate this limitation, but limit the information available to only a single source of inundation at a specific site.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":76259,"startOffsetInUnit":1007},{"endOffsetInDocument":77369,"endOffsetInUnit":2117,"evidenceHash":"7b5e98c1230fe2b168e66dc725eeb79a849b8743e0a47c9ced3637595f86270a","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0009","evidenceText":"This data set was selected due to its large spatial coverage, availability of cross-sections with streamflow information, higher level of sophistication when compared to HAND, engineering scale detail, and a storied use in the literature as an evaluation data set","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":77106,"startOffsetInUnit":1854},{"endOffsetInDocument":80146,"endOffsetInUnit":4894,"evidenceHash":"a6e9559fbeb3f138ef3be95fc27831656a4b2ea24c07b99f53ef3b70a20d2cd6","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0010","evidenceText":"The metrics employed in this study to evaluate inundation extents include CSI, Probability of Detection (POD), and False Alarm Ratio (FAR) and are presented in Equations [6](#page-17-0)[–8,](#page-17-1) respectively.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":79930,"startOffsetInUnit":4678},{"endOffsetInDocument":82213,"endOffsetInUnit":6961,"evidenceHash":"eac2173de226e0a4d7df1f73078936353485bd061e1310b25be833bf60218b93","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0011","evidenceText":"BLE cross sections are intersected with NWM reaches and the median recurrence discharge for 1% and 0.2% levels are selected per NWM V2.1 Full Resolution (FR) flowpaths.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":82045,"startOffsetInUnit":6793},{"endOffsetInDocument":77965,"endOffsetInUnit":2713,"evidenceHash":"76749d3bb553c24e0704711ae5d31e636883b1e824775071968d6a732a520579","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0012","evidenceText":"We selected 49 available HUC8s, shown in Figure [7](#page-16-0), which span about 185 thousand km2 across nine states.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":77847,"startOffsetInUnit":2595},{"endOffsetInDocument":82044,"endOffsetInUnit":6792,"evidenceHash":"7d8e92644647448d94a62069ec08c94301d8c6f92f7cbb9c0f8a33a1f7a07a98","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0013","evidenceText":"Illustrates Base Level Engineering (BLE) cross sections and flowpaths at the HUC8 12100203 near the confluences of West Fork Plum Creek and Clear Fork Plum Creek with Plum Creek.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":81866,"startOffsetInUnit":6614},{"endOffsetInDocument":81061,"endOffsetInUnit":5809,"evidenceHash":"b0d2eee71d5f12b75f68ede4fc98559d8e182c93ecc19ff34afec9229511edbc","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0014","evidenceText":"BLE maps are produced for two recurrence flows, 1% (100 years) and 0.2% (500 years), using 1D HEC-RAS models. The maps are used as benchmarks for validation purposes of OWP FIM.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":80884,"startOffsetInUnit":5632},{"endOffsetInDocument":80535,"endOffsetInUnit":5283,"evidenceHash":"31a6e94b1705b854aaa8346e201e8530dfb4e0242ed6c900db2e0845109c5499","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0015","evidenceText":"To calculate these secondary metrics, one must define three primary metrics starting with true positives (TP) which is predicted wet and wet in the BLE benchmark data set. The two types of errors consist of false positives (FP), or type I errors, which is dry in the benchmark but predicted wet and false negatives (FN), or type II errors, which is wet in the benchmark but predicted dry.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":80147,"startOffsetInUnit":4895},{"endOffsetInDocument":76258,"endOffsetInUnit":1006,"evidenceHash":"b8ee5412ecc9e3c1fc89e0d6274e28f3f9430cbd6d8270074c22a9baa9bba7c6","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0016","evidenceText":"Using the NWM as a source of streamflow input would be a logical choice due to its operational use, but this would introduce hydro-climatic uncertainties that could impact the results of adding a multi-fluvial source extension to HAND.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d8308b9694b4","startOffsetInDocument":76023,"startOffsetInUnit":771},{"endOffsetInDocument":77053,"endOffsetInUnit":1801,"evidenceHash":"2e942cd00dc4712e48458bb165557c84276e70f01676be58eda6cb8a74dde8af","evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0017","evidenceText":"estBFE Viewer","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0033","sectionTitle":"<span id=\"page-15-0\"></span>**2.7. Evaluation**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0033:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"e89e911714dcc026cb0e99c781108537fea524763c5eecf47de4d830